## Proof (Construction)

**Yes, it is possible.** We construct an explicit coloring.

**The coloring.** Define $c:\mathbb{Z}^2\to\{1,2,3\}$ by:
$$c(x,y)=\begin{cases}1 & \text{if } x\equiv 0,\; y\equiv 0 \pmod{2},\\ 2 & \text{if } x\not\equiv y \pmod{2},\\ 3 & \text{if } x\equiv 1,\; y\equiv 1 \pmod{2}.\end{cases}$$

Color 1 = both coordinates even; color 3 = both odd; color 2 = mixed parity.

**Density condition.** Each color appears infinitely often on infinitely many horizontal lines:

- **Color 1:** On every line $y=2k$, all points $(2j,2k)$ have color 1 — infinitely many. Infinitely many such lines exist.
- **Color 2:** On every line $y=k$, the points $(x,k)$ with $x\not\equiv k\pmod{2}$ have color 2 — infinitely many. Infinitely many such lines exist.
- **Color 3:** On every line $y=2k+1$, all points $(2j+1,2k+1)$ have color 3 — infinitely many. Infinitely many such lines exist.

**No rainbow collinear triple.** We show every line in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ contains points of at most two colors. Any line through integer points can be parameterized as
$$(x(t),\,y(t))=(x_0+qt,\;y_0+pt),\quad t\in\mathbb{Z},$$
where $p,q$ are coprime integers with $q\ge 0$ (vertical lines: $q=0,p=1$; horizontal: $p=0,q=1$). The parity pair $(x(t)\bmod 2,\,y(t)\bmod 2)$ determines the color. We analyze by the parities of $p$ and $q$:

**Case 1: $q$ even (hence $p$ odd).** Then $x(t)\equiv x_0\pmod{2}$ is fixed while $y(t)\equiv y_0+t\pmod{2}$ varies. The two parity classes visited are $(x_0\bmod 2,\,y_0\bmod 2)$ and $(x_0\bmod 2,\,1-y_0\bmod 2)$. If $x_0$ is even, these are $(0,0)\to 1$ and $(0,1)\to 2$. If $x_0$ is odd, these are $(1,0)\to 2$ and $(1,1)\to 3$. **At most 2 colors.**

**Case 2: $q$ odd, $p$ even.** Then $y(t)\equiv y_0\pmod{2}$ is fixed while $x(t)\equiv x_0+t\pmod{2}$ varies. The two parity classes are $(x_0\bmod 2,\,y_0\bmod 2)$ and $(1-x_0\bmod 2,\,y_0\bmod 2)$. If $y_0$ is even, these are $(0,0)\to 1$ and $(1,0)\to 2$. If $y_0$ is odd, these are $(0,1)\to 2$ and $(1,1)\to 3$. **At most 2 colors.**

**Case 3: $q$ odd, $p$ odd.** Both parities vary together: $x(t)\equiv x_0+t$, $y(t)\equiv y_0+t\pmod{2}$. The two parity classes are $(x_0\bmod 2,\,y_0\bmod 2)$ and $(1-x_0\bmod 2,\,1-y_0\bmod 2)$. If the starting class is $(0,0)$, the other is $(1,1)$: colors $1$ and $3$. If the starting class is $(1,1)$, the other is $(0,0)$: colors $3$ and $1$. If the starting class is $(0,1)$ or $(1,0)$, the other is $(1,0)$ or $(0,1)$ respectively: both color $2$. **At most 2 colors.**

In every case, each line contains points of at most two colors. Therefore no three collinear points can have three distinct colors. $\blacksquare$

